back to school  at least for now  sri lanka's new president ranil vikram  singha and his government want to show  things are returning to normal  schools have been asked to start on-site  lessons three days a week the other day  students are taught online  but many say the fuel shortage and  economic crisis are still a major  obstacle  i faced a lot of hardships due to the  fuel crisis i'm a mother and a teacher  and i had to queue for two days to get  fuel  we have to wait 30 to 40 minutes for a  three-wheeler taxi and even if we get  one we are helpless with unfair charges  today i paid double the usual fare  the head of sri lanka's teachers union  told al jazeera its members are also  having transport issues  he urged the government to declare  education and essential service giving  it the priority it deserves  schools were closed because there was no  fuel even today that's the situation  with endless cues  as a trade union we don't want schools  to stay closed because the longer that  happens the greater the impact on  students  nearly 30 000 school vans operate around  the country  drivers and other school workers are  hoping the arrangement made this week  will continue  it's best that fuel is continued to be  provided to us through the bus depots  because it's not possible for us to  queue overnight and then drive children  to school in the morning  this is very difficult  fuel is not the only problem  skyrocketing prices have seen school  transport fees double in recent months  and many families have been forced to  look for cheaper options  students have not been spared the  effects of sri lanka's worst economic  crisis in decades but following two  years of pandemic related lockdowns it's  not been easy mine fernandez al jazeera  colombo
 

